# Logitech PRO X TKL LIGHTSPEED (046d:c352) on its cable, vendor HID++
# interface -> uaccess for the seat user; and its node of its own behind its
# LIGHTSPEED receiver (40B0, below), which carries what is typed on it too.
# Not yet checked on this hardware: the ids are from Logitech G HUB's device
# database and Solaar's listing of a cabled unit (issue 3155), read
# 2026-10-21.
#
# Interface 02 on the cable is assumed, as on the G515 TKL and the G915 TKL,
# whose vendor collection (usage page 0xFF00) is interface 02 alone; no
# source gives this keyboard's interfaces. gearbench-ctl report gives each
# node's interface and whether its report descriptor holds HID++, read from
# sysfs, so it needs no access to the node. Gearbench opens interface 02
# alone on the cable, so if HID++ is on another, put a copy of this file in
# /etc/udev/rules.d/ without the c352 line: it overrides this file by name,
# and a reinstall or upgrade leaves it alone. Then run sudo udevadm control
# --reload, replug the keyboard, and paste the report's block into a device
# report. Read access to an interface that carries keystrokes would be a
# keylogger surface.
#
# Matched on properties, as 70-g915.rules is, for the reason it gives. The
# keyboard over Bluetooth has no line here and is not driven.
KERNEL=="hidraw*", SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ENV{ID_VENDOR_ID}=="046d", ENV{ID_MODEL_ID}=="c352", ENV{ID_USB_INTERFACE_NUM}=="02", TAG+="uaccess"

# The PRO X TKL LIGHTSPEED through its LIGHTSPEED receiver (046d:c547): the
# hidraw node of its own, 0003:046D:40B0.<n>, that the kernel's
# hid-logitech-dj gives a device paired to a c547, as 70-g515.rules says of
# the G515 LIGHTSPEED TKL's 40B4 node. Not yet checked on this hardware: the
# 40b0 id and the receiver are from G HUB's device record and Solaar's
# listing of the keyboard's transport ids, read 2026-10-21.
#
# This node carries what is typed on the keyboard as well as its HID++
# traffic, as the G515 LIGHTSPEED TKL's does (70-g515.rules says why). So
# with this line installed, any program the user of the active seat session
# runs can read what is typed on the PRO X TKL, not only Gearbench, which
# drops those reports unread. Granted, as the G515 LIGHTSPEED TKL's node is
# (70-g515.rules). It carries no other device's reports while nothing else
# is paired to the receiver (70-g502x.rules says why).
#
# The grant crosses accounts, as 70-gearbench-uinput.rules says of
# /dev/uinput's: a program that opened the node keeps reading it after another
# account gets the seat, and so reads what that account types on the PRO X
# TKL, a password at the login screen included. On a machine several people
# use, that file's group grant, in a copy of this file in /etc/udev/rules.d/,
# limits the node to the group's accounts. A member then reads it from any
# session, whoever has the seat.
#
# Matched by the HID device's name, as 70-g502x.rules matches the mouse: udev
# gives this node the receiver's properties, so a property match would grant
# the receiver's own interfaces too. KERNELS matches a parent's kernel name,
# which for the paired device is bus:vendor:product.instance.
KERNEL=="hidraw*", SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", KERNELS=="0003:046D:40B0.*", TAG+="uaccess"
